        {
          "t": "p",
          "text": "Dogs were valued as guardians of flock (as we saw in the previous chapter) and home, as pets and as hunters. Dogs were important for hunting, a sport Romans loved—there were even hunts ({i}vēnātiōnēs{/i}) staged in the Circus Maximus and the {i}Amphitheātrum Flāviānum {/i} (also called the {i}Colosseum{/i}, as it was built next to a colossal statue of the first-century AD emperor Nero). Romans saw the hunt as a way for men to display their manliness. Horace called it the “customary work for Roman men, useful for reputation, life and limbs” ({i}Epist.{/i} 1.18.49–50: {i}Rōmānīs sollemne virīs opus, ūtile fāmae{/i}/{i}vītaeque et membrīs{/i}). {i}Grattius{/i}, a contemporary of Ovid (first century BC–first century AD), wrote the {i}Cynēgetica{/i} (τὰ κυνηγετικά) a didactic poem (see below) on hunting dogs, only a small portion of which (540 lines of dacytlic hexameter) survives."
        },
        {
          "t": "p",
          "text": "{i}Varrō{/i} (116–27 BC) in his book about the Latin language ({i}dē Linguā Latīnā{/i}) says that dogs were called {i}canēs{/i} because they sing ({i}canere{/i}) when guarding at night and when hunting. In Cap. XXII, {i}Cavē Canem{/i} (Beware of the Dog), the opening illustration shows a mosiac flooring in the {i}vestibulum {/i} depicting a guard dog. In that chapter, we will also meet one of the family’s dogs: {i}canis {/i} {i}catēnārius{/i} (the guard dog, who was bound with a chain). Such dogs were also used in wall paintings—in {i}Petrōnius’s{/i} {i}Satyricōn{/i} (first century AD), the arriving guests find “not far from the room of the doorkeeper, a huge dog bound with a chain had been painted on the wall and above it was written in capital letters, ‘Beware of the dog’.”{{1}} But dogs were also pets: in this chapter, we see Julia playing with her pet dog (l.77). Cicero relates a story of a father going home to find his youngest daughter in tears over the death of her puppy ({i}dē Dīvīnātiōne{/i}, 1.102). Especially favored by Greeks and Romans alike was a small terrier similar to the Maltese, called {i}Melitaeus{/i} (from the island Melita, modern Malta). Wealthy Romans enjoyed fishponds ({i}piscīnae{/i}) on their estates—both for fresh and salt-water fish, and fish were sometimes tamed and trained to eat from their master’s hand (Cicero, {i}ad Att{/i}. 2.1). There was even a word for someone whose hobby was fish ponds: {i}piscīnārius{/i}! Romans were also partial to pet birds and sometimes had private aviaries ({i}aviāria{/i}). The first-century BC poet Catullus (poems 2, 3) writes about his girlfriend’s grief over her dead sparrow ({i}passer{/i}, quoted in Cap. XXXIV). Ovid ({i}Amōrēs{/i} 2.6) writes about his girlfriend’s dead parrot ({i}psittacus{/i}). Birds are sometimes associated with particular divinities: the eagle ({i}aquila{/i}) was a symbol of {i}Iūppiter{/i} and the peacock ({i}pāvō{/i}) of his wife {i}Iūnō{/i} (you will learn more of {i}Iūppiter{/i} and {i}Iūnō{/i} later). The swan ({i}cȳcnēus{/i}) was the bird of {i}Apollō{/i}, god of light, learning and literature. Apollo’s sister, the huntress {i}Diāna{/i}, is accompanied by her hunting dogs (although she, too, is associated with birds). And {i}Venus{/i}, the goddess of love, rides in a chariot drawn by white doves. In this chapter, you are also introduced to two Roman divinities: {i}Mercurius{/i}, the messenger of the gods, whose winged cap and sandals might be familiar to you from florist advertisements, which display Mercury in flight carrying flowers (rushing for speedy delivery). Mercury carried a herald’s staff, the {i}cadūceum{/i}. In addition to being the {i}deus mercātōrum{/i}, Mercury was the god of thieves, of eloquence and of prosperity. He led the souls of the dead to the underworld. Just as Mercury is associated with the Greek god Hermes, Neptune, an ancient Italian god, later became associated with Poseidon, the Greek god of the sea, and thus of journeys on water. There was a festival to Neptune on July 23 called the {i}Neptūnālia{/i}."
        },
        {
          "t": "p",
          "text": "* Didactic Poetry Didactic poetry is a genre that aims to teach the reader. In the first century BC, Lucretius wrote an epic in six books on Epicurean philosophy ({i}dē Rērum {/i} {i}Nātūrā{/i}); Vergil wrote an epic in four books on farming ({i}Geōrgica{/i})."
        }
